---
intent: >
  Extend the brain.aide body grammar from three to four marker-bounded
  sections by inserting a `studyPlaybook` section between `playbook`
  and `research`. Update the parser, its tagged-result type, and the
  test suite so the parsed `ok` shape carries a fourth `studyPlaybook`
  string field byte-identical to the bytes between
  `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` and
  `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`. All existing three-section
  behavior — verbatim slicing, marker-layout violation classes,
  bytes-outside-pairs silent ignore, frontmatter validation, deprecated
  field rejection, interpolation surface — stays unchanged for the
  prose / playbook / research sections; the new section participates in
  the same closed grammar as a sibling (never parent or child) of the
  other three. Strict-failure migration extends to cover three-pair
  pre-pivot files: a body with prose + playbook + research markers and
  no study-playbook pair returns `malformed-body` naming the missing
  study-playbook markers specifically. No auto-injection, no
  `aide_upgrade` carve-out.
---

## Project Structure

The parser module is a single-helper-exception orchestrator (per the
progressive-disclosure spec): one file, two exports plus one helper,
under ~200 lines of concrete logic. The four-section extension does
not change that shape — it widens an already-closed marker-pair
vocabulary by one entry and adds a fourth body-field destination on
the `ok` result. No new helper subfolders are introduced; the
`extractMarkerSections` helper stays inline above the default export
per the function-ordering rule.

```
src/service/parseBrainAide/
├── .aide                    ← intent spec for this orchestrator (existing — already updated to 4-section grammar)
├── plan.aide                ← this file (new)
├── index.ts                 ← orchestrator (existing — modified)
│                              MARKER_PAIRS: extended from 3 entries to 4 (insert study-playbook between playbook and research)
│                              extractMarkerSections(body: string)
│                                : { kind: "ok"; prose: string; playbook: string; studyPlaybook: string; research: string }
│                                | { kind: "malformed-body"; reason: string }
│                              interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): string[]    (unchanged)
│                              parseBrainAideFromString(content: string): ParseBrainAideResult
│                                (modified — destructures and forwards studyPlaybook into the ok result)
│                              parseBrainAide(root: string): Promise<ParseBrainAideResult>    (default export, unchanged signature)
└── index.test.ts            ← co-located vitest suite (existing — modified)
                               Updates every existing 3-section fixture to include the study-playbook marker pair
                               Adds new test groups for the studyPlaybook section (presence, ordering, missing-pair,
                               malformed/typo, unmatched, nested, slicing, verbatim invariant, three-pair pre-pivot
                               strict-failure migration, per-consumer-ownership type-boundary check)
```

## Prerequisites

This plan touches one file outside the parser folder's scope:

- **`src/types/index.ts`** — must export `ParseBrainAideResult` such that
  the `ok` variant carries a `studyPlaybook: string` field alongside the
  existing `prose: string`, `playbook: string`, `research: string`
  fields. The variant otherwise stays unchanged: `kind: "ok"; config:
  BrainAideConfig; prose: string; playbook: string; studyPlaybook:
  string; research: string`. Field ordering follows marker order
  (prose → playbook → studyPlaybook → research) so JSON serialization
  and human-readable diffs mirror the body layout. JSDoc on the result
  type must list the four marker pairs in required order and mention
  that `studyPlaybook` carries the bytes between
  `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` and
  `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` byte-identical to user input.

  This edit is one-field additive in a shared types file with no
  orchestrator at `src/types/`. Per the architect's decision (see
  Decisions section), this prerequisite is fulfilled by Step 1 of
  this plan rather than forked into a separate plan.aide. The
  plan-aide.md scoping rule is honored by treating `src/types/index.ts`
  as a declared cross-cutting touchpoint with the contract above and
  by limiting this plan's reach into it to exactly the additive
  field on the existing `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` variant — nothing
  else in `src/types/` is modified.

## Plan

### 1. Extend `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` with the `studyPlaybook` field in `src/types/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Add `studyPlaybook: string` to the `ok` variant of `ParseBrainAideResult` in `src/types/index.ts`. Place the field between `playbook: string` and `research: string` so the typed shape mirrors the marker order (prose → playbook → studyPlaybook → research). No other variants change; `missing`, `malformed-frontmatter`, and `malformed-body` stay byte-identical.
- [x] Update the JSDoc block that describes the `ok` branch and lists the recognized marker pairs: it currently enumerates three pairs (prose, playbook, research) in required order — extend the list to four pairs in the new required order, and add one sentence describing what `studyPlaybook` carries (the verbatim bytes between `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` and `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`, byte-identical to user input, no substitution). Update the malformed-body sub-list mention of "missing pair" to clarify that any of the four required pairs missing produces `"missing markers: <opener>, <closer>"`. Update the marker-base name list `prose, playbook, research` wherever it appears in JSDoc to `prose, playbook, studyPlaybook, research`.
- [x] Do NOT touch `BrainAideConfig`, `BrainAideMcpServerConfig`, `BrainState`, `BrainToolResult`, `InfoResult`, or any other type in this file — the schema collapse for those types is settled and out of scope for this plan.
- [x] Run `npx tsc --noEmit` after the edit. Expected: red errors in `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` (the `parseBrainAideFromString` ok-result construction does not yet carry `studyPlaybook`) and in any consumer that constructs an ok result. The parser itself will be fixed in Step 3; consumers that ONLY narrow on `kind` and read existing fields stay green. Do NOT fix the parser or consumers in this step — the type error is the forcing event for the next steps and proves the field is wired into the central type surface.

### 2. Extend `MARKER_PAIRS` and `extractMarkerSections` to the four-pair grammar in `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `.aide/docs/agent-readable-code`

This step is a single coupled unit — the constant declaration and its consumer (the helper that walks the body) live in the same file and must be updated together so the file compiles and the section-extraction logic produces the new four-section result shape in one edit. Lettered sub-steps run in one implementor session.

- [x] 2a. Extend `MARKER_PAIRS` from three entries to four. Insert a new entry between the existing `playbook` and `research` entries: `{ token: "studyPlaybook", open: "<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->", close: "<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->" }`. Keep the existing `as const` annotation and tuple ordering rules; the array is positional (per the `as const` literal-tuple narrowing) and the prose-then-playbook-then-studyPlaybook-then-research order is the source of truth for both the section-order check and the missing-marker enumeration order. Update the JSDoc above `MARKER_PAIRS`: change the count from "six recognized marker tokens grouped as three pairs" to "eight recognized marker tokens grouped as four pairs"; rename the literal-union narrowing description from `"prose" | "playbook" | "research"` to `"prose" | "playbook" | "studyPlaybook" | "research"`; reaffirm that token strings double as ok-result destination field names so the marker base name and the typed key are the same word (`study-playbook` in markers, `studyPlaybook` in the typed result — kebab-to-camel transformation is purely surface, not a code path).
- [x] 2b. Update the `extractMarkerSections` return type annotation to add `studyPlaybook: string` between `playbook: string` and `research: string` on the `ok` branch. Update the `MarkerEntry.sectionToken` literal-union narrowing from `"prose" | "playbook" | "research"` to `"prose" | "playbook" | "studyPlaybook" | "research"`. Update the helper's JSDoc to reflect the four-pair count: change "all six recognized markers" to "all eight recognized markers", change "the three openers must appear in prose-then-playbook-then-research order" to "the four openers must appear in prose-then-playbook-then-studyPlaybook-then-research order", and update the nesting-class summary to mention four sibling sections (never parent-child). The pipeline order itself (Step 1: presence; Step 2: match; Step 3: order; Step 4: nesting; Step 5: slice) does not change — every step iterates over `MARKER_PAIRS`, so adding the fourth entry extends each step automatically.
- [x] 2c. Update the final ok-return at the bottom of `extractMarkerSections` to include `studyPlaybook: result["studyPlaybook"]!` between `playbook` and `research`. The slicer (`for (const span of pairSpans) result[span.token] = body.slice(...)`) populates `result` from the iterated `MARKER_PAIRS`, so this is the only literal-key reference that needs editing. Confirm by reading the file end-to-end that no other location enumerates the three section tokens by literal name — if any unsuspected enumeration exists (a switch statement, a hardcoded array literal `["prose", "playbook", "research"]`, a destructuring pattern), update it to four sections in the same edit. Per the spec's "single closed-grammar walker" invariant, all such enumerations should derive from `MARKER_PAIRS` itself; the audit verifies that.
- [x] 2d. Confirm the malformed-marker scan regexes (`candidateRegexA` and `candidateRegexB`) do NOT need to enumerate the section base names. Read both regex literals: pattern A is `/<!--[^>]*aide[^>]*(?:prose|playbook|research|start|end)[^>]*-->/gi` (a candidate matches if it contains `aide` AND any of the listed words); pattern B is `/<!--\s*(?:prose|playbook|research)[^>]*(?:start|end)[^>]*-->/gi`. Both currently list `prose|playbook|research` as the base-name alternation. Decision: extend both regexes by adding `study-playbook` (pattern A) and `study-playbook` (pattern B) to the alternation so malformed variants of the new section's marker (e.g. `<!-- AIDE-STUDY-PLAYBOOK-START -->`, `<!-- aide-study-playbook-strart -->`) are caught by the same scan that catches malformed prose / playbook / research markers. The alternation must use the kebab-case `study-playbook` (the marker form), not the camelCase `studyPlaybook`. Pattern A's full word-list becomes `(?:prose|playbook|study-playbook|research|start|end)`. Pattern B's word-list becomes `(?:prose|playbook|study-playbook|research)`. The pattern still relies on the body containing literally `aide` and one of those words for pattern A, or starting with one of those base names + a start/end keyword for pattern B — adding the new word does not loosen either pattern.

### 3. Wire `studyPlaybook` through `parseBrainAideFromString` and update its JSDoc

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `.aide/docs/progressive-disclosure`

- [x] Update the destructure of `bodyResult` in `parseBrainAideFromString` from `const { prose, playbook, research } = bodyResult` to `const { prose, playbook, studyPlaybook, research } = bodyResult`. Update the final ok-return shape to include `studyPlaybook` between `playbook` and `research`: `{ kind: "ok", config, prose, playbook, studyPlaybook, research }`. Field ordering in the returned object literal mirrors the marker order so human-readable diffs and JSON serialization both reflect the body layout.
- [x] Update the JSDoc on `parseBrainAideFromString`. Step 5's description currently says "Extract the three required named body sections via the closed-vocabulary marker walker" and lists "prose then playbook then research" — change "three" to "four" and the order list to "prose then playbook then studyPlaybook then research". Step 6's description currently says "Return the ok result with all three body sections alongside the frontmatter config" — change "three" to "four". The validation order (1: required frontmatter fields; 2: deprecated fields; 3: body grammar) is unchanged.
- [x] Update the JSDoc on the default-exported `parseBrainAide`. The "ok" branch description enumerates the three required marker pairs by name and shape; extend the enumeration to four pairs in the new required order and add the new pair: `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`. The "malformed-body" branch description references "every absent marker" — the wording does not need to change because the helper now scans four pairs by virtue of `MARKER_PAIRS`; add one sentence noting that the new section participates in the same closed grammar as a sibling of the other three (never parent or child). The load-bearing-invariants list currently references "prose, playbook, and research" being byte-identical to file content — extend to "prose, playbook, studyPlaybook, and research".
- [x] Run `npx tsc --noEmit`. Expected: clean exit. The Step 1 type error in this file is now resolved; consumers that did not enumerate body fields by name remain unaffected. Do NOT modify any other file in this step (consumer updates are separate plans for separate modules per the AIDE-pipeline-is-code-only handoff scoping). If `tsc` reports errors in any consumer file (e.g. `tools/brain`, `service/install/provisionBrain`, `cli/sync`), the plan is incomplete — re-read the spec's per-consumer-ownership outcomes (the brain tool reads `prose` only; `provisionBrain` reads `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, `research`; sync reads frontmatter only). Adding a new optional field on the ok variant must not break consumers that read existing fields. If a consumer breaks, it has reached into a section it does not own — surface that in the implementor's hand-back, do NOT silently fix it here.

