### 5. Add new `studyPlaybook`-specific test groups for behaviors unique to the four-section grammar

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

These tests exercise behaviors that exist ONLY because the body grammar gained a fourth section. They cover:

- The strict-failure migration extension for three-pair pre-pivot files (the most load-bearing new behavior — the spec's outcome #7 calls it out explicitly and dedicates a "Good examples" block to it).
- The `parseBrainAideFromString` parity coverage for the new section.
- A `noStudyPlaybookContent` fixture for the missing-pair parity test parallel to the existing `noPlaybookContent` fixture.

- [x] Add a new describe block `3i' — strict-failure migration: pre-pivot three-pair body` (placed immediately after the existing `3i — strict-failure migration: pre-pivot heading-based body` describe to keep the migration-policy tests grouped). The single test asserts that a brain.aide with valid frontmatter and a body containing prose + playbook + research marker pairs in the right relative order — but missing the study-playbook pair entirely — returns `malformed-body` with reason exactly `"missing markers: <!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->"`. The test fixture must be a valid three-pair body (the implementor builds it inline by concatenating the three marker-pair blocks from `MARKER_OPENERS` / `MARKER_CLOSERS` in the right order). The assertion comment must reference the spec's `outcomes.desired` strict-failure-migration outcome that names this case ("a `brain.aide` carrying the prior three-pair marker-bounded body returns `malformed-body` with a reason naming the missing study-playbook markers specifically"). The assertion comment must also state that the parser does NOT auto-detect the three-pair shape, does NOT auto-rewrite, does NOT silently default the missing studyPlaybook section to an empty string, and that there is no `aide_upgrade` carve-out — strict failure is the only resolution path.
- [x] Update the existing `3i — strict-failure migration: pre-pivot heading-based body` test's expected reason. The current fixture has frontmatter + heading-based body (no markers). The current expected reason lists six markers (prose pair + playbook pair + research pair). Update it to list eight markers in the `MARKER_PAIRS` iteration order: prose-start, prose-end, playbook-start, playbook-end, study-playbook-start, study-playbook-end, research-start, research-end. The literal expected reason becomes: `"missing markers: <!-- aide-prose-start -->, <!-- aide-prose-end -->, <!-- aide-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-playbook-end -->, <!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->, <!-- aide-research-start -->, <!-- aide-research-end -->"`. The test's narrative comment also needs updating to reflect "all eight missing markers" instead of "all six missing markers".
- [x] Add a new test inside `3n — parseBrainAideFromString parity` for the studyPlaybook-pair-missing case. Mirror the existing `noPlaybookContent` fixture and test exactly: build a `noStudyPlaybookContent` string with frontmatter + prose + playbook + research marker pairs (no study-playbook pair), run both `parseBrainAide` from disk and `parseBrainAideFromString` from memory against it, assert both return `kind: "malformed-body"`, both reasons are exactly `"missing markers: <!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->"`, and `expect(fromDisk).toEqual(fromString)`. This proves the disk and string paths agree on the new pair's missing-pair detection.

### 6. Run the full test suite and verify clean type-check

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] Run `npx tsc --noEmit` from the project root. Expected exit code 0. If any consumer outside `src/service/parseBrainAide/` fails to type-check, surface the file and error to the user without fixing it — the per-consumer-ownership invariant says the brain tool reads `prose` only, the provisioner reads `playbook` + `studyPlaybook` + `research`, sync reads frontmatter only; consumers that did not enumerate body fields by name should remain green. A consumer failure means it reached into a section it does not own, which is a separate plan.aide cycle for that module (per the parser-foundation-first sequencing in the handoff).
- [x] Run `rtk vitest run src/service/parseBrainAide/` (or `npx vitest run src/service/parseBrainAide/`). Expected: every test passes — both the original three-section tests (now updated to four-section fixtures) and the new study-playbook-specific tests. If any test fails, do NOT modify the test to make it pass; trace back to the production code (steps 2–3) and confirm the `MARKER_PAIRS` extension, the regex updates, the destructure update, and the ok-result construction match what the test asserts. The test is the contract; the production code adapts to the spec's outcomes via the test's expectations.
- [x] Run `rtk vitest run` (project-wide). Expected: every project-wide test passes. If any consumer test fails (e.g. a `provisionBrain/index.test.ts` test that constructs an ok-shape inline), surface to the user and let them choose the remediation: either it's a legitimate new fixture-update need belonging to the next module's plan.aide cycle, or it indicates a consumer reaching into the parser's internals via the result shape. Do not silently fix consumer tests in this plan.

## Decisions

### 1. Type edit lives inline as Step 1, not as a separate plan.aide

The handoff names `src/types/index.ts` as part of the parser refactor scope. The edit is one additive line on `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` plus one JSDoc paragraph. `src/types/` has no orchestrator, no helper subfolders, no `.aide` spec — the "single-module scoping" rule was written for module folders that DO have an orchestrator and DO benefit from a per-folder plan.aide. Forking a separate plan for one field on a shared types file would create a one-line plan.aide that cross-references this plan immediately, doubling the orchestration overhead for zero architectural clarity. The architect treats the types file as a declared cross-cutting touchpoint with the contract listed under `## Prerequisites` and limits this plan's reach into it to exactly one field on one variant — nothing else. If the implementor finds itself touching anything else in `src/types/`, it has scope-crept and must hand back to the architect.

### 2. `MARKER_PAIRS` insertion at index 2 (between playbook and research) is positional and intentional

The constant is `as const`-narrowed to a literal tuple. Iteration order is the source of truth for: the section-order check (Step 3 of the helper's pipeline), the missing-marker enumeration order (Step 1), the openerOrder slice extraction, the unmatched-opener-vs-unmatched-closer reporting order, and the JSON serialization order of the ok-result fields when they're constructed in marker order. Placing the new entry at index 2 (after playbook, before research) means every downstream consumer of `MARKER_PAIRS` automatically sees the four-pair grammar in the right order. Placing it at index 3 (at the end) would put research before study-playbook in every error message, in every iteration, and in every contract surface — wrong by the spec. Placing it at index 0 or 1 would produce equally wrong order. The single source of truth for "what is the required section order" is this array's index ordering; every step of `extractMarkerSections` derives from it. Future additions (a fifth section, a sixth) follow the same rule: pick the array index that matches the required position in the body, edit one constant, every downstream check picks up the new entry by virtue of iterating `MARKER_PAIRS` rather than enumerating section names by literal.

### 3. Field name is `studyPlaybook` (camelCase, no `Hub` suffix) on the typed result

The spec's frontmatter explicitly names this rule (outcomes.undesired field-name regression: `playbookHub`, `studyPlaybookHub`, `researchHub` are forbidden). The typed key matches the marker token base name: kebab-case in markers (`study-playbook`), camelCase in the typed result (`studyPlaybook`), the same word in both surfaces. The kebab-to-camel transformation is purely a TypeScript-identifier surface concern and does not appear in any code path — the `MARKER_PAIRS` constant carries both the marker string AND the camel-case token; the slicer populates a `Record<string, string>` keyed by the camel token; the ok-result destructure reads the camel keys.

### 4. Field placement on `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` is positional (between `playbook` and `research`) for diff and serialization legibility

TypeScript does not care about field declaration order semantically; an object is structurally typed. But human-readable diffs (when a future maintainer reads the `ok` variant declaration), JSON serialization output (`JSON.stringify(result)`), and Vitest snapshot output all reflect declaration order. Placing `studyPlaybook` between `playbook` and `research` mirrors the marker order and keeps the surface coherent end-to-end. The same positional intent governs the field order in the destructure inside `parseBrainAideFromString` and the field order in the returned object literal.

### 5. Section-order validator extension is automatic — no code changes needed beyond `MARKER_PAIRS`

The existing `extractMarkerSections` Step 3 (section-order check) reads `expectedTokenOrder` from `MARKER_PAIRS.map((p) => p.token)`. Extending `MARKER_PAIRS` to four entries automatically extends `expectedTokenOrder` to a four-element array; the comparison logic (`if (currentExpectedIdx > nextExpectedIdx)`) is index-based and works for arrays of any length. The same reasoning applies to Step 1 (presence check, iterates `MARKER_PAIRS`), Step 2 (unmatched-opener / unmatched-closer, iterates `MARKER_PAIRS`), Step 4 (nesting check, iterates `pairSpans` which is built from `MARKER_PAIRS`), and Step 5 (slicing, iterates `pairSpans`). The architect's audit per Step 2c verifies this — the only literal three-section enumerations should be the JSDoc comment text (which Step 2 updates explicitly) and the final `return { kind: "ok", prose: result["prose"]!, playbook: result["playbook"]!, research: result["research"]! }` (which Step 2c updates). If the audit finds another enumeration the plan didn't anticipate, the implementor surfaces it instead of guessing.

### 6. Strict-failure migration scope: extend the missing-pair detection naturally; the regression-prevention test is explicit

Per spec outcome #7 (strict-failure migration covers BOTH pre-pivot shapes — heading-based AND three-pair). The heading-based case is already covered by the existing `3i` describe (its expected reason now lists eight markers instead of six — Step 5's first bullet handles that one-line update). The three-pair case is NEW; Step 5's main work is adding `3i'` with the explicit `"missing markers: <!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->"` assertion. The test exists not because the code has a special path for "is this a three-pair file" — there is no such path; the missing-pair detection naturally enumerates `MARKER_PAIRS` and flags every absent pair. The test exists to PIN the regression-prevention contract in code: if a future "helpful" maintainer adds a three-pair-tolerant fallback (the spec's "Bad examples" block calls this out by name), the test fails immediately. The test's narrative comment must explain that this is regression prevention against the auto-injection anti-pattern, not a documentation-only assertion.

### 7. Malformed-marker scan regex extension uses kebab-case `study-playbook` in the alternation

Both regex literals (`candidateRegexA` and `candidateRegexB`) match the LITERAL marker bytes in the body string, not the typed identifier. The bytes are kebab-case (`<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->`), so the alternation must list `study-playbook` — not `studyPlaybook`. The pattern still requires `aide` AND a recognized base name in pattern A, OR starts-with one of the base names + a start/end keyword in pattern B; adding the new word maintains both invariants. Tested by Step 4's malformed-marker case extension (uppercase variant + typo variant for the new section).

### 8. Test additions stay grouped with related existing tests (no "study-playbook tests" appendix block)

Per the architect's planning rule (decision point 6 in the briefing): NEW tests for the new section live next to related existing tests. The "studyPlaybook missing pair only" case lives in `3e` next to "missing prose pair only", not in a separate `3p — studyPlaybook tests` block. The "typo in study-playbook section name" case lives in `3f` next to "typo in section name (playboook)". The "study-playbook before playbook" case lives in `3g`. The "study-playbook pair nested inside playbook pair" case lives in `3h`. The "scratch text between playbook closer and study-playbook opener" case lives in `3j`. The "studyPlaybook containing literal ${name}" case lives in `3k`. The exception is the strict-failure migration extension in `3i'`, which is its own describe parallel to the existing `3i` so the migration-policy tests stay grouped — these are conceptually peer regression-prevention contracts, not parameterized cases of one another.

### 9. JSDoc updates are content-shape changes, not surface-doc rewrites

Every JSDoc that enumerates the body sections needs to enumerate four sections instead of three; every count of "three pairs" / "six markers" / "three sections" becomes "four pairs" / "eight markers" / "four sections". The architect identifies the locations (Steps 1, 2a, 2b, 3) and describes the change shape (extend the enumeration, update the count). The implementor writes the actual JSDoc text following the existing voice and length of each block. No JSDoc text is transcribed into this plan because the playbook-driven JSDoc convention (orchestrator JSDoc summarizes the full flow, helper JSDoc explains why-it-exists) is what governs the wording — and the implementor has direct playbook access via the `study-playbook` skill.

### 10. The `interpolateArgs` helper and the `parseBrainAide` default-export I/O wrapper are unchanged

`interpolateArgs` operates on `BrainAideConfig` (frontmatter only) and never touches body sections — extending the body grammar does not add any interpolation surface. `parseBrainAide` (the I/O wrapper) reads the file, hands the bytes to `parseBrainAideFromString`, and returns whatever the string parser returns; its function signature is unchanged. The only edits to either are JSDoc enumeration extensions (Step 3's third bullet covers the default export's JSDoc; `interpolateArgs` JSDoc does not enumerate body sections at all so it stays untouched).
